.de LI
.LIST
.SHIFT_LIST 10p
..
.PARA_SPACE 1m
.TITLE "\s+(10A Guide to This System\s0"
.AUTHOR "\s+5eth-man\s0"
.DOCTYPE    DEFAULT
.COPYSTYLE  FINAL
.PRINTSTYLE TYPESET
.PT_SIZE 10
.START
.PP
Use vim keys (\f(CWh/j/k/l\fP) to move around this document.
\f(CWs\fP fits it to the window width (\f(CWa\fP to undo that),
\f(CWK\fP and \f(CWJ\fP zoom in and out,
\f(CWf\fP numbers the links so you can follow one by typing its number and \f(CWEnter\fP,
and \f(CWq\fP quits.
\f(CWi\fP recolours the page into the desktop's theme, for reading at night.
(These are \f(CWzathura\fP's keys, the PDF reader.)
.LI
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+F1\fP shows this document at any time.
.ITEM
\f(CWMod\fP is the Super key, the one with the Windows logo on it.
.LIST OFF
.PP
This is a fork of Luke Smith's LARBS that stayed on i3 when upstream moved to
dwm, and grew up doing day-to-day sysadmin work.
Anything marked \fBfork\fP below does not exist upstream.
Questions or ideas? Open an issue:
.LI
.ITEM
This fork:
.PDF_WWW_LINK "https://github.com/eth-man/voidrice" "github.com/eth-man/voidrice"
.ITEM
The installer:
.PDF_WWW_LINK "https://github.com/eth-man/larbs" "github.com/eth-man/larbs"
.ITEM
The status bar and its icons:
.PDF_WWW_LINK "https://github.com/eth-man/ablocks" "github.com/eth-man/ablocks"
.ITEM
Upstream:
.PDF_WWW_LINK "https://github.com/LukeSmithxyz/voidrice" "Luke Smith's voidrice"
.LIST OFF
The FAQs are at the end.
.HEADING 1 "Welcome!"
.HEADING 2 "What this system is trying to be"
.LI
.ITEM
\fBNaturalness\fP \(en
everything important is a few keypresses away, so you stop thinking about the
computer and think about the work.
.ITEM
\fBEconomy\fP \(en
small programs, light on resources, mostly in the terminal, each one easy to
read and change.
.ITEM
\fBVim keys everywhere\fP \(en
your hands never need to leave the home row.
.ITEM
\fBCalm\fP \(en
\fBfork:\fP the screen stays quiet until something needs you.
Almost everything is drawn in one text colour, grey means off, and red means
look at me.
If something on screen is red, it is the thing to deal with.
.LIST OFF
.HEADING 2 "Keyboard changes"
.LI
.ITEM
Caps Lock is Escape when tapped and another Super key when held.
Vim gets a lot nicer.
.ITEM
The Menu key (between the right Alt and Ctrl) is a second Super key, for
one-handed use on a laptop.
.LIST OFF
Both live in \f(CW~/.local/bin/tools/remaps\fP if you would rather not.
The shell uses vi mode; \f(CW~/.inputrc\fP is where to turn that off.
.HEADING 2 "The status bar"
.PP
\fBfork:\fP the bar is \f(CWablocks\fP, a small C replacement for i3blocks
written for this setup.
Workspaces are on the left; on the right are the blocks: weather, CPU, volume,
brightness, battery, the date and time, bluetooth, network traffic, latency,
VPN, wifi and the keyboard layout, plus blocks that only appear when they have
something to say (failed services, pending upgrades, a recording in progress).
.PP
Every block is clickable, and the buttons mean the same thing everywhere:
.LI
.ITEM
\fBLeft click\fP \(en the obvious action: open the mixer, the network menu, the calendar.
.ITEM
\fBMiddle click\fP \(en details, or show and hide a number.
.ITEM
\fBRight click\fP \(en a note explaining what the icons mean.
.ITEM
\fBScroll\fP \(en up and down, where that means something (volume, brightness).
.LIST OFF
.PP
The icons are \fBsuckless icons\fP, this fork's own icon font: each one is a few
lines of drawing code on one grid, so they all match.
Things move a little when it matters: the battery fills as the charger goes in,
the wifi arcs drain when the connection drops, changed digits on the clock roll
over like a flip clock.
Several blocks hide their numbers until they change, then fade them away again.
.PP
The blocks are compiled into ablocks, in \f(CW~/ablocks/config.h\fP.
Each one runs a script from \f(CW~/.local/bin/statusbar/\fP, and
\f(CW~/.local/bin/SCRIPTS.md\fP describes every script in the system.
\f(CW~/.config/i3blocks/config\fP has the same blocks for plain i3blocks, kept as a
fallback.
.HEADING 2 "Themes"
.PP
\fBfork:\fP the whole desktop takes its colours from one theme: the bar, window
borders, the terminal, dmenu, notifications, GTK programs and zathura.
Switching is one command:
.LI
.ITEM
\f(CWretheme gruvbox\fP \(en warm retro brown, the old st colours
.ITEM
\f(CWretheme nord\fP \(en calm blue-grey
.ITEM
\f(CWretheme -m\fP \(en pick one from dmenu
.ITEM
\f(CWretheme -l\fP \(en list them
.LIST OFF
Themes are plain files in \f(CW~/.config/theme/\fP.
To make your own, copy one, change the colours and run \f(CWretheme\fP with its name.
Saving a theme in vim applies it straight away.
Terminals that are already open keep their colours until you open a new one.
.HEADING 2 "Learning more"
.PP
\f(CWgetkeys\fP shows the key bindings of a program you name.
\f(CWMod+Shift+e\fP picks a tutorial video on a program or concept to watch.
.HEADING 1 "Key Bindings"
.PP
All of these live in \f(CW~/.config/i3/config\fP (\f(CWcfi\fP in the terminal opens it).
Shortcuts are case-sensitive: \f(CWMod+Q\fP means \f(CWMod+Shift+q\fP.\c
.FOOTNOTE
Yes, people have asked how to type a capital letter now that Caps Lock is Escape.
Hold Shift.
.FOOTNOTE OFF
.HEADING 2 "Windows"
.LI
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Enter\fP \(en terminal (\f(CWst\fP; \f(CWman st\fP for its keys)
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+Enter\fP \(en terminal in the same directory as the focused one
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+q\fP \(en close the window
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+q\fP \(en kill it, for when closing doesn't work
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+d\fP \(en dmenu, to run anything by name
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+h/j/k/l\fP \(en move focus (the arrow keys work too)
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+h/j/k/l\fP \(en move the window
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Ctrl+h/j/k/l\fP \(en move the workspace to another screen
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+y/u/i/o\fP \(en resize the window
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+t\fP \(en switch between splitting side by side and stacked\c
.FOOTNOTE
The focused window's border has one side in a different shade: that is where
the next window will open.
.FOOTNOTE END
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+/\fP \(en new terminal below; \f(CWMod+'\fP \(en new terminal beside
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+f\fP \(en fullscreen
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+f\fP \(en distraction-free mode: every binding sleeps until you press it again
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+Space\fP \(en float the window; \f(CWMod+Space\fP \(en move focus between floating and tiled
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+o\fP \(en make a floating window sticky, so it follows you between workspaces
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+b\fP / \f(CWMod+Shift+n\fP \(en float the window small in the bottom left/right corner, for a video on the side
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+r\fP \(en resize a floating window to an exact size
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+s\fP / \f(CWMod+Shift+s\fP \(en more/less space between windows
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+z\fP / \f(CWMod+Shift+z\fP \(en more/less space around the edge
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+d\fP \(en no gaps; \f(CWMod+Shift+t\fP \(en gaps back to normal
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+b\fP \(en hide or show the bar
.LIST OFF
.HEADING 2 "Workspaces"
.LI
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+(number)\fP \(en go to that workspace; \f(CWMod+Shift+(number)\fP \(en send the window there
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Tab\fP or \f(CWMod+\e\fP \(en back to the workspace you were just on
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+g\fP / \f(CWMod+;\fP \(en the workspace to the left/right (also \f(CWPage Up\fP/\f(CWPage Down\fP)
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Home\fP / \f(CWMod+End\fP \(en the first/last workspace
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+Delete\fP \(en the panic button: mutes everything, pauses all music
and video, and jumps to an empty workspace with \f(CWhtop\fP and a file manager
open, so the screen looks like work.
.LIST OFF
.HEADING 2 "Programs"
.LI
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+w\fP \(en web browser
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+r\fP \(en ranger, the file manager
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+u\fP \(en a dropdown terminal (the same key hides it again)
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+a\fP \(en a dropdown calculator (the same key hides it again)
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+i\fP \(en htop, what the machine is doing
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+y\fP \(en calcurse, calendar and schedule
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+n\fP \(en newsboat, RSS
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+v\fP \(en your vimwiki
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+m\fP \(en \fBfork:\fP pick a markdown note from dmenu and edit it
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+a\fP \(en pulsemixer, the audio mixer
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+e\fP \(en email in neomutt, once it is set up (see the FAQ)
.LIST OFF
.HEADING 2 "Music and volume"
.PP
Music is mpd, so these work from anywhere, whatever is focused.
.LI
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+p\fP \(en play/pause; \f(CWMod+Shift+p\fP \(en pause
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+<\fP / \f(CWMod+>\fP \(en previous/next track; \f(CWMod+Shift+<\fP \(en restart the track
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+[\fP / \f(CWMod+]\fP \(en back/forward 10 seconds (with Shift, 2 minutes)
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+-\fP / \f(CWMod++\fP \(en volume down/up 5% (with Shift, 15%)
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+m\fP \(en mute
.LIST OFF
The media keys on the keyboard do what they say.
.HEADING 2 "Screenshots and recording"
.PP
Everything is saved in your home directory, named after the moment it was taken,
so nothing is ever overwritten.
.LI
.ITEM
\f(CWPrint\fP \(en screenshot of everything
.ITEM
\f(CWShift+Print\fP \(en pick an area or window, and save it or copy it
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Print\fP \(en record the screen, audio or webcam, chosen from dmenu; an icon shows on the bar while it runs
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Delete\fP \(en stop the recording
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+c\fP \(en webcam in the corner of the screen, for screencasts
.LIST OFF
.HEADING 2 "System"
.LI
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Backspace\fP \(en the session menu: lock, sleep, hibernate, reboot, shut down, restart i3, leave i3, screen off
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+x\fP \(en lock the screen
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+x\fP / \f(CWMod+Shift+Backspace\fP \(en shut down / reboot (both ask first)
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+Escape\fP \(en leave i3 (asks first)
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+F1\fP \(en this document; \f(CWMod+Shift+F1\fP \(en \fBfork:\fP check the whole setup for problems (see the FAQ)
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+F2\fP \(en restart i3 in place
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+F3\fP \(en choose which screens to use; \f(CWMod+Shift+F3\fP \(en \fBfork:\fP apply a saved screen layout, for docking
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+F4\fP \(en sleep (asks first)
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+F5\fP \(en restart NetworkManager
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+F6\fP \(en the torrent client (starts its daemon first if needed)
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+F7\fP \(en \fBfork:\fP turn the stock ticker on or off
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+F9\fP / \f(CWMod+F10\fP \(en mount/unmount a USB drive or phone; \f(CWMod+Shift+F9\fP \(en \fBfork:\fP mount a network share
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+F11\fP \(en search the web
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+F12\fP \(en nmtui, for networks; \f(CWMod+Shift+w\fP \(en \fBfork:\fP a quicker wifi picker in dmenu
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+`\fP \(en pick an emoji or symbol and copy it
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Insert\fP \(en show what is on the clipboard
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+c\fP \(en \fBfork:\fP the clipboard plumber (below)
.LIST OFF
.HEADING 1 "What This Fork Adds"
.HEADING 2 "Networking and sysadmin"
.LI
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Ctrl+s\fP \(en pick a host from \f(CW~/.ssh/config\fP and ssh to it.
\f(CWsac <alias> [<user>@]<host> [-p <port>]\fP adds one.
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Ctrl+v\fP \(en WireGuard: connect, disconnect or switch tunnel.
The tunnels in \f(CW/etc/wireguard\fP hold private keys and are root-only, so it asks for
your password.
The VPN block names the tunnel while one is up; middle click it for the endpoint,
handshake age and traffic.
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Shift+v\fP \(en pick a Pritunl profile.
.ITEM
\f(CWMod+Ctrl+p\fP \(en what is listening on which port.
.LIST OFF
.HEADING 2 "The clipboard plumber"
.PP
\f(CWMod+c\fP looks at whatever you have selected and offers what can be done with
it: open or play a link, play a video in a floating window, turn a MAC address into
Cisco, Windows or Unix notation or look up its vendor, make a QR code.
To add your own action, put a guarded line defining a shell function in
\f(CWcabl\fP; the menu lists the function names, so name it what you want to see.
.HEADING 2 "Status bar blocks you won't find upstream"
.LI
.ITEM
\fBFailed\fP \(en failed systemd services, system and user.
Invisible until something fails; click to see what.
.ITEM
\fBLatency\fP \(en round-trip time, with a notification when it gets bad.
.ITEM
\fBTraffic\fP \(en up and down rates; fast transfers stream across the bar.
.ITEM
\fBVPN\fP \(en WireGuard and OpenVPN, with the tunnel's name.
.ITEM
\fBBluetooth\fP \(en off, blocked, scanning or connected, with the device's icon
and battery when it reports one.
.ITEM
\fBUpgrades\fP \(en how many packages can be upgraded; click to upgrade.
.ITEM
\fBTasks\fP \(en jobs queued with the task spooler.
.ITEM
\fBScreen timeout\fP \(en how long until the screen blanks; right click to change it.
.ITEM
\fBRepo\fP \(en whether these dotfiles have changes to pull, push or commit
(optional, off on some machines).
.LIST OFF
.HEADING 1 "Bookmarks"
.PP
\f(CW~/.config/bmdirs\fP and \f(CW~/.config/bmfiles\fP list directories and files you
use a lot, each with a short key.
The \f(CWshortcuts\fP script turns them into shell aliases and file manager keys.
With \f(CWd\fP for \f(CW~/Documents\fP, for example:
.LI
.ITEM
\f(CWd\fP in the shell \(en \f(CWcd\fP to \f(CW~/Documents\fP
.ITEM
\f(CWgd\fP in ranger \(en go there; \f(CWtd\fP \(en open it in a new tab
.ITEM
\f(CWmd\fP / \f(CWYd\fP in ranger \(en move/copy the selected files there
.LIST OFF
A key in \f(CWbmfiles\fP becomes an alias that opens that file in your editor.
By Luke's convention the config files start with \f(CWcf\fP: \f(CWcfi\fP for i3,
\f(CWcfv\fP vim, \f(CWcfz\fP zsh, \f(CWcfx\fP xresources, \f(CWcfd\fP dunst,
\f(CWcfab\fP the bar's blocks, \f(CWcfs\fP your ssh hosts and \f(CWcft\fP the theme in use.
\f(CWcf\fP is only for config files: the theme command is simply \f(CWtheme\fP, as in
\f(CWtheme nord\fP, and Tab completes its names, like the other tools of this fork.
Saving either file in vim regenerates everything, and new shells and ranger
windows pick it up.
Just don't give a bookmark the name of a command you need.
.HEADING 1 "Frequently Asked Questions"
.HEADING 2 "Something seems broken. Where do I start?"
.PP
\fBfork:\fP press \f(CWMod+Shift+F1\fP, or run \f(CWvoidrice-doctor\fP.
It looks for the problems that otherwise fail silently: a script that isn't
executable, a bar block that errors, an icon the font doesn't have, a key bound to
something that isn't installed, colours out of step with the theme, and dotfiles
that have drifted from the repository.
.HEADING 2 "My keyboard isn't doing what I expect!"
.PP
The remaps in \f(CW~/.local/bin/tools/remaps\fP may be overriding your own settings;
comment out what gets in the way.
Keyboard layouts and other per-machine bindings go in
\f(CW~/.config/i3/config.local\fP, which i3 reads if it exists and which is never
committed.
.HEADING 2 "My audio isn't working!"
.PP
Audio can be finicky on a fresh install.
Restart the audio server or reboot, and pick the output you want in
\f(CWpulsemixer\fP (\f(CWMod+Shift+a\fP).
.HEADING 2 "How do I copy and paste?"
.PP
In graphical programs, \f(CWCtrl+c\fP and \f(CWCtrl+v\fP as usual.
In the terminal those keys mean other things; \f(CWman st\fP has the terminal's own.
Vim uses the system clipboard, so what you yank there pastes anywhere.
.HEADING 2 "How do I change the wallpaper?"
.PP
Run \f(CWsetbg\fP on an image, or hover over it in ranger and type \f(CWbg\fP.
It is kept as \f(CW~/.config/wall.png\fP.
.HEADING 2 "How do I change the colours?"
.PP
\fBfork:\fP with a theme; see \fIThemes\fP above.
Don't edit \f(CW~/.config/x11/colors\fP, dunst's \f(CWpalette.conf\fP, GTK's
\f(CWgtk.css\fP or zathura's \f(CWpalette\fP: \f(CWretheme\fP writes all of them from
the theme and will overwrite your changes.
.HEADING 2 "How do I make the terminal font bigger?"
.PP
\fBfork:\fP no need to rebuild st: it reads \f(CW*.font\fP from
\f(CW~/.config/x11/xresources\fP.
Change the \f(CWpixelsize\fP there, save it in vim (which reloads it) and open a new
terminal.
Comment lines in that file with \f(CW!\fP, never \f(CW/*\fP: the file goes through the
C preprocessor, and a stray \f(CW/*\fP silently swallows everything after it.
That once ate the font line and kept the font tiny for months.
.HEADING 2 "How do I set up email?"
.PP
Email is optional: install
.PDF_WWW_LINK "https://github.com/lukesmithxyz/mutt-wizard" "mutt-wizard"
(\f(CWyay -S mutt-wizard-git\fP) and add an account with \f(CWmw add\fP.
Then \f(CWMod+e\fP opens your mail, \f(CWMod+F8\fP syncs it, \f(CWmw cron\fP syncs it
regularly, and the mailbox block counts what is unread.
.HEADING 2 "How do I set up music?"
.PP
mpd plays whatever is in \f(CW~/Music\fP (change it in \f(CW~/.config/mpd/mpd.conf\fP).
After adding music, run \f(CWmpc update\fP.
\f(CWncmpcpp\fP in a terminal browses and queues it.
.HEADING 2 "How do I update or commit my dotfiles?"
.PP
\fBfork:\fP the repository is \fBnot\fP your home directory.
It lives in \f(CW~/voidrice-repo\fP, and \f(CW$HOME\fP is not a git work tree at all.
It used to be, which meant a single \f(CWgit add -A\fP could have published
\f(CW~/.ssh\fP, saved browser passwords and wallet data.
.PP
The files therefore exist in two places, and \f(CWdotsync\fP keeps them in step:
.LI
.ITEM
\f(CWdotsync status\fP \(en which tracked files differ between \f(CW$HOME\fP and the repo
.ITEM
\f(CWdotsync diff\fP \(en the differences themselves
.ITEM
\f(CWdotsync capture\fP \(en copy your live edits into the repo, ready to commit
.ITEM
\f(CWdotsync deploy\fP \(en copy the repo back out to \f(CW$HOME\fP
.LIST OFF
.PP
Both directions only show what they would do until you add \f(CW-y\fP.
They only ever move files git already tracks, so a private file can't sneak into
the repository.
The usual loop: edit in \f(CW$HOME\fP so the change is live,
\f(CWdotsync capture -y\fP, commit in \f(CW~/voidrice-repo\fP.
Programs always read from \f(CW$HOME\fP, so if you edited in the repo,
\f(CWdotsync deploy -y\fP before reloading, or you'll reload the old file and
wonder why nothing changed.
.HEADING 2 "Where is the source for st, dmenu and the bar?"
.PP
\fBfork:\fP kept, not thrown away.
\f(CWst\fP and \f(CWdmenu\fP are in \f(CW~/.local/src/\fP: edit \f(CWconfig.h\fP and
\f(CWsudo make install\fP.
The bar is in \f(CW~/ablocks\fP: edit \f(CWconfig.h\fP,
\f(CWmake PREFIX=$HOME/.local install\fP, then \f(CWMod+F2\fP.
Its icon font is drawn by \f(CWicons/icons.py\fP in the same place.
.HEADING 1 "Credit"
.PP
The foundation is Luke Smith's LARBS: this fork keeps his structure and most of his
scripts, and the credit for those is his.
The parts marked \fBfork\fP are mine, and so are their mistakes.
.LI
.ITEM
.PDF_WWW_LINK "https://github.com/eth-man/voidrice" "github.com/eth-man/voidrice"
\(en these dotfiles
.ITEM
